qx|A2|st||rh||ah|Board 3 IMPs   -   On October 23, 2024|pn|hondo717,aspiring,toasterln,MN08|
md|1SQ32HJ43DT632CAJ5,SK9HT876DA94CK972,SAT654HK9DKJ5CQT8,SJ87HAQ52DQ87C643|sv|e|
mb|P|mb|P|mb|1S|mb|P|mb|2S|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Normally you don't want to let the opponents play in 2@S with an 8-card fit. So East might have made a balancing double over 2@S. Letting the opponents play at the 2-level when the points are evenly spread is not winning bridge in general. On the other hand, with 4-3-3-3 I understand why East passed.|
PG| ***** 28 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BEast: ^*NCan I double with 9 points?|
PG| ***** 29 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In balancing seat, after you have passed once, yes. You are just competing at that point. But pass was not terrible either.|
PG| ***** 30 ***** |


pc|D7|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In general, you should avoid leading away from honors unless you have no better lead. Your best lead is from a weak suit with no honors, the longer the better. So on this hand, I would have led a club as East.|
