mb|1D|mb|P3N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|3N is a possible bid although, that bid supposed to show 13-15 and East has 17. So it is a bit of an underbid. Since we play 2/1 GF, East could bid 2@C. The Bergen style of 2/1, which I like, says that even with 4 clubs and a 4-card major, you bid 2@C to establish the game force.|
PG| ***** 47 ***** |


ub||
PG| ***** 48 ***** |


mb|2C|mb|P?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The 2@C by East is not denying a 4-card major, so West must bid 2@H here to make sure they don't miss a 4-4 major suit fit.|
PG| ***** 49 ***** |


mb|2H|mb|P?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now East can bid 2N, and then bid 4N over 3N as a quantitative invitation. That is one advantage of 2/1 GF. It allows you to take a lot of bids to describe slam possible hands.|
PG| ***** 50 ***** |


mb|2N|mb|P|mb|3N|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|4N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|4N here shows 16-18 or so asking West to go on with basically 16 points or more.|
PG| ***** 51 ***** |


